<text>
   <body>
      <div type="voynich_team">
         <listPerson>
            <person xml:id="ebb">
               <persName>Elisa Beshero-Bondar</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>Elisa</forename>
                  <surname>Beshero-Bondar</surname>
               </persName>
               <occupation type="scholar">
                  <roleName>Program Chair, <ref
                        target="https://behrend.psu.edu/school-of-humanities-social-sciences/academic-programs/digital-media-arts-and-technology"
                        >Digital Media, Arts, and Technology (DIGIT)</ref>, Professor of Digital
                     Humanities, and Director of the <ref target="https://newtfire.org">Digital
                        Humanities Lab</ref> at <affiliation>Penn State Erie, The Behrend
                        College</affiliation>
                  </roleName>
               </occupation>
               <note>An active member of the Text Encoding Initiative (TEI), she has served since
                  2016 on the <ref target="http://www.tei-c.org/Activities/Council/">TEI Technical
                     Council</ref>, an eleven-member international committee that supervises
                  amendments to the <ref target="https://tei-c.org/guidelines/P5/">TEI
                     Guideline</ref>s.</note>
            </person>
            <person xml:id="hjb">
               <persName>Hadleigh Jae Bills</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>Hadleigh</forename>
                  <forename>Jae</forename>
                  <surname>Bills</surname>
                  <roleName>Research Assistant</roleName>
               </persName>
               <note resp="#hjb">Hadleigh Jae Bills is a student at Penn State Behrend. She is
                  working towards a B.A. in Digital Media, Arts, and Technology.</note>
            </person>
         </listPerson>
      </div>
      <div type="voynich_people">
         <listPerson>
            <person xml:id="elv">
               <persName>Ethel Voynich</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>Ethel</forename>
                  <forename>Lilian</forename>
                  <surname type="paternal">Boole</surname>
                  <surname>Voynich</surname>
                  <roleName>Researcher</roleName>
               </persName>
               <note resp="#hjb">Irish novelist, musician and revolutionary married to <persName
                     type="inline" ref="#wmv">Wilfrid Voynich</persName>. Studied the botanical
                  aspects of the manuscript.</note>
               <note>
                  <ptr
                     target="https://ies.sas.ac.uk/research/research-projects/cultivate-mss-project/cast-characters/ethel-lilian-voynich-1864-1960-novelist-scholar"
                  />
               </note>
            </person>
            <person xml:id="hth">
               <persName>Herman Theodor Holm</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>Herman</forename>
                  <forename>Theodor</forename>
                  <surname>Holm</surname>
                  <roleName>Botanist</roleName>
               </persName>
               <note resp="#hjb">A botanist who built his career around studying plant life across
                  places like Greenland, Colorado, and the United States. After immigrating to the
                  U.S., he worked as a botanist for institutions like the United States National
                  Museum and the Department of Agriculture, continuing his focus on plant research.
                  He later earned his doctorate from the Catholic University of America and
                  eventually returned there as a professor of biological research.</note>
               <note>
                  <ptr target="https://findingaids.lib.catholic.edu/repositories/2/resources/176"/>
                  <ptr
                     target="https://findingaids.lib.catholic.edu/repositories/2/resources/176/collection_organization"
                  />
               </note>
            </person>
            <person xml:id="hon">
               <persName>Hugh O'Neill</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>Hugh</forename>
                  <surname>O'Neill</surname>
                  <roleName>Botanist</roleName>
               </persName>
               <note resp="#hjb">A botanist from the Catholic University of America. Identified
                  plants in 1944, but what he identified are plants from the Americas, but the
                  Voynich Manuscript came before Christopher Columbus' voyages.</note>
               <note>
                  <ptr target="https://voynichlanguage.fandom.com/wiki/Hugh_O%27Neill"/>
               </note>
            </person>
            <person xml:id="rzn">
               <persName>René Zandbergen</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>René</forename>
                  <surname>Zandbergen</surname>
                  <roleName>Researcher</roleName>
               </persName>
               <note resp="#hjb">René Zandbergen, originally a satellite navigation engineer for the
                  European Space Agency, became deeply involved in studying the mysterious Voynich
                  Manuscript on the side. Starting in 1995, he joined an online community focused on
                  analyzing the manuscript’s strange and unreadable text. Over time, he helped
                  develop a standard way to transcribe its characters and created one of the most
                  widely used websites dedicated to researching the manuscript.</note>
               <note>
                  <ptr target="https://independent.academia.edu/RenéZandbergen"/>
               </note>
            </person>
            <person xml:id="tcp">
               <persName>Theodore Petersen</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>Theodore</forename>
                  <surname>Petersen</surname>
                  <roleName>Transcriptor</roleName>
               </persName>
               <note resp="#hjb">Fr. Petersen first made a full photocopy of the Voynich
                  manuscript’s photostats, then created a handwritten copy of the manuscript,
                  consulting the original in a New York safe deposit vault when passages were
                  especially difficult. He finished this work in 1944, adding notes on possible
                  meanings, marking unusual character sequences, highlighting common words, and
                  suggesting plant identifications in the herbal section based in part on Holm and
                  O’Neill’s research. He also compiled a complete concordance of the manuscript’s
                  words, though the writer had not seen it. Petersen remained a leading Voynich
                  expert until his death, after which his papers were passed to Friedman,
                  inventoried by Tiltman, and eventually housed at the George C. Marshall Library
                  and Archives in Lexington, Virginia.</note>
               <note>
                  <ptr target="https://www.voynich.nu/solvers.html#petersen"/>
               </note>
            </person>
            <person xml:id="wmv">
               <persName>Wilfrid Voynich</persName>
               <persName>
                  <forename>Wilfrid</forename>
                  <surname>Voynich</surname>
                  <roleName>Bookseller</roleName>
               </persName>
               <note resp="#hjb">A polish bookseller who bought the Voynich Manuscript from a Jesuit
                  library in Italy.</note>
               <note>
                  <ptr
                     target="https://www.lindahall.org/about/news/scientist-of-the-day/wilfrid-voynich/"
                  />
               </note>
            </person>
         </listPerson>
      </div>
